  "query_preview": "..(After midnight Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 700 AM EDT FRI AUG 14 2026\n\nThe previous long term discussion for Sunday onward follows:\n\nSunday should bring drier weather, although things won't stay dry\nfor long. Additional ridging building aloft will make Sunday the \nhottest day of the week, with highs reaching the low to mid 90s \nacross the forecast area. Given continued humidity, more \nwidespread triple digit apparent temperatures may be possible \nSunday afternoon; current LREF output suggests over a 30 percent \nchance of exceeding a 100 degree apparent temperature for the \nnorthern portions of our forecast area by only 2 PM on Sunday. \nThankfully, this heat will be short lived. Additional upper level \ntroughing will be digging into the Great Lakes region by Sunday \nafternoon, and a shortwave rotating through the base of this \ntrough should work to flatten the upper level ridge somewhat by \nSunday night. Meanwhile, a surface low will be located near \nMichigan, with a cold front extending to the southwest. As the \nsystem translates eastward, this front will approach Kentucky by \nearly Monday morning, bringing the next round of rain. As in \nprevious days, moisture will be quite high (with westerly flow \nsupporting PW values generally in the range of 1.8-1.9 inches, \napproximately the 90th percentile), and at least isolated \ninstances of flash flooding will be possible. Thus, WPC has placed\nthe northern and eastern portions of the forecast area in a \nMarginal (Level 1/4) Risk for Excessive Rainfall on Monday. The \nmagnitude of the flash flood risk will remain fairly dependent on \nhow saturated soils are going into the event, in addition to the \ntiming of this frontal passage (which itself will modulate how \nwidespread any convection is); therefore, uncertainty remains \nhigh. Regardless, rain appears likely on Monday.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, temperatures will be much more\ntolerable. With less influence from the upper level ridge, MaxTs\nwill generally range from the mid to upper 80s across the forecast\narea, with the hottest temperatures expected to the southwest.\nMoisture will be noticeably lower too, with dewpoints generally\nfalling into the upper 60s across the forecast area. That being\nsaid, the general pattern will still be fairly active. Upper level\ntroughing over the Great Lakes will persist, while ridging will\ndominate across the southern CONUS, with models generally\ndepicting the ridge axis in the Four Corners region or slightly\nfarther east in western Texas. This will place eastern Kentucky in\na region of either quasi zonal or northwest flow aloft, depending\non which model you believe; either way, this will open the door\nfor additional rain chances through the end of the period.",
